            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around ceiling light fixtures. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 

Air leakage was also observed through the rangehood exhaust fan. I recommend you replace your exhaust fan with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

There was quite a bit of air leakage observed around the frames of the two front windows. I recommend removing the trim and sealing using caulking, and/or low expansion sprayfoam if there is a larger gap present. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $ 80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6.0 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. Based on our discussions, about 30% of your walls are also strapped with R6 on the inside. You mentioned of plans to add the IS3000 (R20) Energy Wall system to the exterior walls of the home, please see comment below. 



Savings:

By increasing your walls by a R20, you could potentially save $ 420 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 8,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 

Please note, the IS3000 system was mentioned during our discussions. The IS3000 product carries a total R value of 18.8, which would qualify for $$5,000 in rebates. 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have 10 inches (R32) of fiberglass batt insulation.  I have modelled adding an additional 2.5 inches (R8) of blown cellulose insulation, for a total insulation value of R40. I have modelled an upgrade to R40 as compression and ventilation may be an issue by adding more, due to the heel height and slope of the current roof. 



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R40, you could potentially save $ 80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ. There are no rebates for upgrading an R32 attic by R8. </ceilings>
                <windows>

As mostly all of your windows are newer double pane ones that do appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass. 

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the one remaining wood framed window in the livingroom with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified window rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



Savings: 

By replacing the wood framed window with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $ 40 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.4 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $ 250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</windows>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 83% AFUE that is sealed combustion, which vents exhaust gas directly outdoors and draws in 100% outside air for the burner. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 83% AFUE, you could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ. There are no rebates available for an 83% AFUE furnace. 



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

WOODSTOVE

Savings (with current oil furnace):

By using your current wood stove for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy savings. This assumes a cost of $ 100 per cord of wood for harvesting your own. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
